//! run
//! link: ffi/helpers.c
//! signal: ILL
//! signal-macos: TRAP
//! stdout:
//| before
import extern;
// A call to a `noreturn` function is followed by `call void @llvm.trap()` and
// `unreachable` (D19.7), which is `ud2` on x86-64 and `brk #1` on arm64, so a
// callee that returns anyway dies by SIGILL, or by SIGTRAP on a Mac, with no
// message (D11.4). The helper is a separate
// translation unit declared `noreturn` on this side only, so nothing but the
// trap stands between the call and the next statement: this is the one
// runtime-observable consequence of D19.7. It is a live witness and not only
// a mirror of the emitted-text assertions in bootstrap0/test/gen_call_test.c: deleting
// the `call void @llvm.trap()` in gen_expr.c and leaving the `unreachable`
// alone makes this test die by SIGSEGV instead, so it goes red.
import std.rt;

// C: helper_returns_anyway, test/lang/ffi/helpers.c, which returns at once, although this
// declaration says noreturn: the test wants the trap after the call.
// gives: no result. writes: no fort storage. keeps: no pointer. calls: no fort function. frees:
// nothing.
extern fn helper_returns_anyway() noreturn;

// C call helper_returns_anyway: no argument.
fn main() i32 {
    println("before");
    // The trap ends the program without flushing the runtime's buffers, as an
    // abort does (D11.4), so what must be seen is flushed first.
    rt.flush_all();
    helper_returns_anyway();
}
